---
title: What is Persistent Homology?
date: 2026-07-10
slug: what-is-persistent-homology
css: ../css/ph-widgets.css
---

Persistent homology is the tool I reach for when I want to ask data a question that statistics cannot phrase: *what shape are you?* This post is the ten-minute version, with toys. Everything below is computed live in your browser — click things. If you want the full guided tour (the Vietoris–Rips complex, the ℤ/2 linear algebra, stepping through the actual matrix reduction), I made a separate full-screen version: [The Shape of a Point Cloud](https://brezgis.com/ph-explainer/).

## The question statistics can't ask

Here are twenty-four measurements — two features of some cells, two sensor readings, whatever you like. Your eye needs a tenth of a second to notice the important thing about them: *these points sit on a loop.*

<figure class="ph-fig" id="ph-shape">
<div class="fig-panel"><canvas id="shape-canvas" class="plane" height="360" aria-label="A scatter of points arranged in a rough ring."></canvas></div>
<div class="controls" role="group" aria-label="Summaries to overlay">
<button class="chip" data-overlay="mean">the average</button>
<button class="chip" data-overlay="trend">the trend line</button>
<button class="chip" data-overlay="eye">what your eye sees</button>
</div>
</figure>

The average lands in the one place where there is *no* data. The trend line shrugs. Means, variances, and regressions summarize *where* the data is; none of them can say the sentence "there is a hole in the middle." That sentence is a statement about **shape**, and the branch of math that makes it precise is topology. Persistent homology is topology turned into something you can run on a matrix.

Why expect data to have a shape at all? Because measurements are usually many, but the dials behind them are few — a face photographed under a turning light is a million pixels, yet only one angle truly varies. This is the *manifold hypothesis*: high-dimensional data tends to lie near a low-dimensional shape, because the process that generated it had only a few degrees of freedom. Anything periodic traces a loop. Shapes are what generating processes look like from the outside.

## Give the dust a body

A finite set of points is topologically just dust. The classic trick: grow a disk of radius *r* around every point and study the union of disks instead. Drag the slider.

<figure class="ph-fig" id="ph-grow">
<div class="fig-panel"><canvas id="grow-canvas" class="plane" height="380" aria-label="Points with growing disks around them, merging into blobs as the radius increases."></canvas></div>
<div class="controls">
<label>radius <em>r</em>
<input type="range" id="grow-r" min="0" max="0.30" step="0.001" value="0.02">
</label>
<span class="readout" id="grow-read">r = 0.02</span>
</div>
</figure>

Small *r*: dust. Large *r*: one featureless blob. Somewhere in between, the union of disks briefly *becomes the shape your eye saw*. So which radius is the right one? Here is the idea that makes the whole subject work: **refuse to choose.** Sweep *r* from zero to huge and keep a diary of every topological event along the way — when each feature appears (its *birth*) and when it disappears (its *death*). That diary is called a **barcode**.

## Reading a barcode

The simplest features are connected components, counted by H<sub>0</sub>. At *r* = 0 every point is its own island; as disks grow, islands merge, and each merge ends one island's story. Draw each lifespan as a horizontal bar:

<figure class="ph-fig" id="ph-merge">
<div class="fig-panel">
<canvas id="merge-canvas" class="plane" height="330" aria-label="Two clusters of points with a few stragglers, with disks growing as the slider moves."></canvas>
<canvas id="merge-bars" height="190" aria-label="Barcode of connected components. Each bar is one component's lifespan. Hover a bar to highlight its component."></canvas>
</div>
<div class="controls">
<label>radius <em>r</em>
<input type="range" id="merge-r" min="0" max="0.32" step="0.001" value="0.015">
</label>
<span class="readout" id="merge-read">r = 0.015</span>
<span class="spacer"></span>
<span class="counter">hover a bar ⤴</span>
</div>
</figure>

**Hover a bar** to light up the island whose life it records. The short bars die young — points that quickly joined a neighbor; that is the texture of noise. The two long bars are the two genuine clusters. And one bar never dies: at the end everything is a single blob, and that component lives forever.

The more interesting characters are the holes themselves, counted by H<sub>1</sub>. A loop is *born* at the radius where a ring of disks first closes, and *dies* at the radius where its middle fills in:

<figure class="ph-fig" id="ph-loops">
<div class="controls" role="group" aria-label="Choose a point cloud">
<button class="chip active" data-shape="ring">one ring</button>
<button class="chip" data-shape="eight">figure eight</button>
<button class="chip" data-shape="random">random scatter</button>
</div>
<div class="fig-panel">
<canvas id="loops-canvas" class="plane" height="330" aria-label="A point cloud with edges and filled triangles appearing as the radius grows."></canvas>
<canvas id="loops-bars" height="150" aria-label="Barcode of loops. Hover a bar to highlight the loop it records."></canvas>
</div>
<div class="controls">
<label>radius <em>r</em>
<input type="range" id="loops-r" min="0" max="0.30" step="0.001" value="0.06">
</label>
<span class="readout" id="loops-read">r = 0.06</span>
<span class="spacer"></span>
<span class="counter">hover a bar ⤴</span>
</div>
</figure>

Switch to the figure eight: two holes, two long bars. Switch to random scatter: little loops flicker into existence by accident and are filled almost immediately. This is the entire reading lesson: **long bars are features of the data; short bars are accidents of the sampling.** And it comes with a warranty — the *stability theorem* says that perturbing the points a little moves no bar's endpoints by more than a little, which is why you may trust a barcode computed from noisy data.

Under the hood, none of this needs the plane. The algorithm only ever reads *pairwise distances*, builds a growing family of simplicial complexes from them, and reduces one boundary matrix over ℤ/2 — the whole subject is a null space and a quotient, which is exactly why I can point it at data in ℝ<sup>1000</sup>. If you want to see the matrix reduction happen column by column (it is genuinely satisfying), that is §7–8 of [the full tour](https://brezgis.com/ph-explainer/).

