</span>
</div>
<figcaption>Hover any bar or diagram point to see its feature in the plane. H<sub>0</sub> in the lighter blue, H<sub>1</sub> in the darker.</figcaption>
</figure>

## Why I care

In my own work the "point cloud" is usually the latent space of a language model — token or sentence embeddings, attention patterns, activations. Those spaces are high-dimensional, noisy, and have no ground-truth geometry, but they plausibly *do* have shape: the processes that generate language have structure, and the manifold hypothesis says that structure should leave fingerprints. Topology is attractive here precisely because it needs no alignment between spaces — you can compare the shape of two embedding spaces without ever putting them in the same coordinates.

## Resources

Tools I use for persistent homology on model internals. I maintain forks for most of these, because they are often not built for LLM analysis — the data is noisy and does not have any ground-truth representation the way biological data does.

- [eff-ph](https://github.com/berenslab/eff-ph) — effective PH pipelines; evaluated on toy examples and biological data with ground truth.
- [flooder](https://github.com/plus-rkwitt/flooder) — fast GPU-friendly filtration construction for large point clouds.
- [HOLE](https://github.com/FoxHound0x00/hole) — visualizing the topology of neural nets in H<sub>0</sub>; originally evaluated on image-classification networks.
- [PH on attention maps](https://github.com/angelinatsai04/mitll_clinic/tree/adam_spring) — persistent homology applied to transformer attention maps.

TDA for NLP is a niche but quickly growing area. Start with Adaku Uchendu and Thai Le's survey, [Unveiling Topological Structures from Language: A Comprehensive Survey of Topological Data Analysis Applications in NLP](https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.10298), and her companion list, [AwesomeTDA4NLP](https://github.com/AdaUchendu/AwesomeTDA4NLP).

And for the mathematics itself: Robert Ghrist's essay [Barcodes: the persistent topology of data](https://www2.math.upenn.edu/~ghrist/preprints/barcodes.pdf) is the classic next step; Edelsbrunner & Harer's *Computational Topology* is the standard textbook; [Ripser](https://github.com/Ripser/ripser) is what the professionals use.

